lemma_low_degree_lefschetz_isomorphisms.proof:
{
  "lemma_id": "lemma_low_degree_lefschetz_isomorphisms",
  "cited_lemmas": [
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_437"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_438"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_448"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_hard_lefschetz_reduces_to_injectivity"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_gysin_projection_formula_all_degrees"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_gysin_trace_compatibility"}
  ],
  "cited_references": [],
  "proof": "Fix an integer \\(t\\ge 1\\), a smooth projective geometrically connected variety \\(X\\) of pure dimension \\(d\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_{q^t}\\), and an \\(\\mathbb F_{q^t}\\)-rational very ample divisor \\(H\\) on \\(X\\). Put \\(L=\\mathcal O_X(H)\\); very ampleness implies ampleness. Fix a nonzero element of \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell(1)\\) and use it throughout to identify Tate twists with \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\). Let \\(Q=q^t\\). Apply lemma_437 to \\(X\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_Q\\) and the ample line bundle \\(L\\). It gives integers \\(m\\ge 1\\) and \\(r\\ge 1\\) and a section over \\(\\mathbb F_{Q^m}\\) of \\(L^{\\otimes r}\\) whose zero scheme \\(Y\\) is a nonempty effective ample Cartier divisor, smooth and geometrically connected of pure dimension \\(d-1\\) over \\(\\mathbb F_{Q^m}\\). Set \\(M=tm\\), so \\(\\mathbb F_{Q^m}=\\mathbb F_{q^M}\\). Let \\(X_M=X_{\\mathbb F_{q^M}}\\) and let \\(i:Y\\hookrightarrow X_M\\) be the closed immersion. Since \\((X_M)_k\\cong X_k\\), it suffices to prove the statement for \\(X_M\\). The base change \\(H_M\\) of \\(H\\) to \\(X_M\\) is an \\(\\mathbb F_{q^M}\\)-rational very ample divisor; let \\(\\xi=\\mathrm{cl}_{X_M}(H_M)\\) and let \\(\\eta=\\mathrm{cl}_Y(H_M|_Y)\\). The restriction of a very ample line bundle to a closed subscheme is very ample, so \\(H_M|_Y\\) is very ample on \\(Y\\). Functoriality of cycle classes gives \\(\\eta=i^*\\xi\\). By the hypothesis of the lemma applied with \\(t=M\\), \\(V=Y\\), and the very ample divisor \\(H_M|_Y\\), for every \\(j\\) with \\(0\\le j\\le d-1\\) the map \\(L_Y^{(d-1)-j}:H^j_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(Y_k,\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\to H^{2(d-1)-j}_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(Y_k,\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\) is an isomorphism. Fix \\(n\\) with \\(0\\le n\\le d-2\\). By lemma_448 applied to \\(Y\\) (dimension \\(d-1\\)) and the class \\(\\eta\\), the bilinear pairing \\(B_Y^n(a,b)=\\operatorname{tr}_Y(\\eta^{d-1-n}a\\cup b)\\) on \\(H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(Y_k,\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\) is perfect. By lemma_438, \\(i^*:H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\to H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(Y_k,\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\) is an isomorphism. It remains to compare this pairing with the corresponding pairing on \\(X_M\\). Since \\(Y\\) is the zero scheme of a section of \\(L^{\\otimes r}\\), \\(\\mathcal O_{X_M}(Y)\\cong L^{\\otimes r}\\), hence \\(\\mathrm{cl}_{X_M}(Y)=r\\xi\\). For the regular codimension-one immersion \\(i\\), the degree-zero Gysin map \\(i_!\\) satisfies \\(i_!1=\\mathrm{cl}_{X_M}(Y)\\) (this is the Thom-class characterization of the Gysin map for an effective Cartier divisor). Thus \\(i_!1=r\\xi\\). For \\(x,y\\in H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\), set \\(z=\\xi^{d-n-1}\\cup x\\cup y\\in H^{2d-2}_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\). By the projection formula in lemma_gysin_projection_formula_all_degrees, \\(i_!(i^*z)=z\\cup i_!1=r\\xi^{d-n}\\cup x\\cup y\\). By the trace compatibility for Gysin maps (lemma_gysin_trace_compatibility; equivalently the \\(b=1\\) case of the defining property of \\(i_!\\) in lemma_gysin_projection_formula_all_degrees), \\(\\operatorname{tr}_{X_M}(i_!a)=\\operatorname{tr}_Y(a)\\) for every \\(a\\in H^{2d-2}_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(Y_k,\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\). Applying this to \\(a=i^*z\\) yields \\(\\operatorname{tr}_{X_M}(\\xi^{d-n}x\\cup y)=r^{-1}\\operatorname{tr}_Y(i^*z)=r^{-1}\\operatorname{tr}_Y(\\eta^{d-1-n}i^*x\\cup i^*y)=r^{-1}B_Y^n(i^*x,i^*y)\\). Since \\(r\\neq 0\\) in \\(\\mathbb Q_\\ell\\), \\(i^*\\) is an isomorphism, and \\(B_Y^n\\) is perfect, it follows that \\(B_X^n(x,y)=\\operatorname{tr}_{X_M}(\\xi^{d-n}x\\cup y)\\) is a perfect pairing on \\(H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\). Indeed, if \\(B_X^n(x,y)=0\\) for all \\(y\\), then \\(B_Y^n(i^*x,i^*y)=0\\) for all \\(y\\); since \\(i^*\\) is surjective and \\(B_Y^n\\) is perfect, \\(i^*x=0\\), hence \\(x=0\\), and the argument for the second variable is identical. By lemma_448 applied to \\(X_M\\) and the very ample class \\(\\xi\\), perfectness of \\(B_X^n\\) implies that \\(L^{d-n}:H^n_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\to H^{2d-n}_{\\acute{\\mathrm{et}}}(X_{M,k},\\mathbb Q_\\ell)\\) is injective. By lemma_hard_lefschetz_reduces_to_injectivity, injectivity implies that this map is an isomorphism. Since \\(n\\) was arbitrary with \\(0\\le n\\le d-2\\), the required conclusion holds for \\(X_M\\), and hence for the original \\(X\\

). Since the finite base extension does not change the geometric fibre over \\(\\overline{\\mathbb F}_q\\), the isomorphism proved for \\(X_M\\) is exactly the required isomorphism for \\(X\\). This completes the proof."
}

lemma_483.proof:
{
  "lemma_id": "lemma_483",
  "cited_lemmas": [
    {"user_id": "eoinm", "lemma_id": "lemma_207"},
    {"user_id": "eoinm", "lemma_id": "lemma_77"},
    {"user_id": "Spark", "lemma_id": "lemma_projective_top_degree_vanishing"}
  ],
  "cited_references": [],
  "proof": "Let A = X ×_{F_q} X and let p_1,p_2:A->X be the projections. We use the Gysin pushforward convention of eoinm lemma_207: for a morphism f:U->V of smooth projective geometrically connected varieties over F_q of dimensions D and E, the map f_* = f_!:H^m(U_k,Q_l)->H^{m+2E-2D}(V_k,Q_l) is characterized by tr_V(f_*u cup v)=tr_U(u cup f^*v), where the traces are the normalized traces of eoinm lemma_77 after the fixed identification of Tate twists with Q_l. The uniqueness of this map follows from the perfectness of the Poincare pairings in eoinm lemma_77: if two maps have the same trace pairing against every v, their difference pairs trivially with every v and hence is zero. The maps denoted pr_{1*} and pr_{2*} in the statement are identified with these Gysin maps for p_1 and p_2.\n\nFirst note that A is smooth projective geometrically connected over F_q. Smoothness and projectivity are stable under products. Base change to k=bar(F_q) gives A_k = X_k ×_k X_k. Since X is geometrically connected and smooth over F_q, X_k is smooth and connected over the algebraically closed field k. A smooth connected finite type scheme over a field is irreducible: regular local rings are domains, so distinct irreducible components cannot meet, and connectedness leaves only one component. Since k is algebraically closed, the product of irreducible finite type k-schemes is irreducible; hence A_k is connected. Thus eoinm lemma_77 applies to X and to A, giving normalized traces tr_X:H^{2d}_et(X_k,Q_l)->Q_l and tr_A:H^{4d}_et(A_k,Q_l)->Q_l with tr_Y(cl(y))=[kappa(y):F_q] for closed points y of Y.\n\nIt suffices to prove the identity for integral codimension d cycles Z; the general rational case follows by Q_l-linearity of cycle classes and of the operations. Let c=cl(Z) in H^{2d}_et(A_k,Q_l). Fix n and alpha in H^n(X_k,Q_l), beta in H^{2d-n}(X_k,Q_l). If n<0, then H^n(X_k,Q_l)=0 by convention; if n>2d, then H^n(X_k,Q_l)=0 by Spark lemma_projective_top_degree_vanishing. In these cases both sides are zero. Assume 0<=n<=2d.\n\nSet gamma=p_1^*alpha cup c in H^{n+2d}_et(A_k,Q_l) and delta=p_2^*beta cup c in H^{4d-n}_et(A_k,Q_l). Then Z_*(alpha)=p_{2*}gamma and Z^dagger_*(beta)=p_{1*}delta.\n\nFor the left hand side, apply the adjunction formula for p_2:A->X with u=gamma and v=beta. Since dim A=2d and dim X=d, p_{2*}gamma in H^n(X_k,Q_l), and lemma_207 gives tr_X(Z_*(alpha) cup beta)=tr_X(p_{2*}gamma cup beta)=tr_A(gamma cup p_2^*beta)=tr_A(p_1^*alpha cup c cup p_2^*beta).\n\nFor the right hand side, apply the adjunction formula for p_1:A->X with u=delta and v=alpha. We have p_{1*}

delta in H^{2d-n}_et(X_k,Q_l), and lemma_207 for p_1 gives tr_X(p_{1*}delta cup alpha)=tr_A(delta cup p_1^*alpha). By graded commutativity, alpha cup p_{1*}delta = (-1)^{n(2d-n)} p_{1*}delta cup alpha. Put s=(-1)^{n(2d-n)}. Hence tr_X(alpha cup Z^dagger_*(beta))=s tr_A(delta cup p_1^*alpha)=s tr_A(p_2^*beta cup c cup p_1^*alpha). Since c has degree 2d, moving p_1^*alpha past c contributes sign (-1)^{2dn}=1, and moving it past p_2^*beta contributes s; therefore p_2^*beta cup c cup p_1^*alpha=s p_1^*alpha cup c cup p_2^*beta. Thus tr_X(alpha cup Z^dagger_*(beta))=s^2 tr_A(p_1^*alpha cup c cup p_2^*beta)=tr_A(p_1^*alpha cup c cup p_2^*beta). Comparing with the left hand side computation gives tr_X(Z_*(alpha) cup beta)=tr_X(alpha cup Z^dagger_*(beta)). This completes the proof for integral Z and hence for rational Z by Q_l-linearity."}
}
